TI  - Effectiveness of non-pharmaceutical control strategies in highly pathogenic avian influenza (H5N1, H5N8) outbreaks
PY  - 2025
T2  - Clinical Infection in Practice
VL  - 27
C7  - 100504
DO  - 10.1016/j.clinpr.2025.100504
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105012972771?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Research, Medical Research Circle (MedReC), Goma, Democratic Republic Congo
AB  - Highly pathogenic avian influenza (HPAI) viruses H5N1 and H5N8 pose significant threats to animal and human health worldwide. In the absence of universally effective vaccines, non-pharmaceutical control strategies (NPCS) including surveillance, biosecurity, vaccination, traceability, communication, and preventive culling play a critical role in outbreak management, particularly when integrated via the One Health approach. This article assesses the effectiveness of NPCS across varied geographic and socio-economic contexts, drawing on recent epidemiological data and case studies from the United States, Cambodia, France, sub-Saharan Africa, Indonesia, and other affected regions. Countries with strong health infrastructure, like France, implement coordinated, multi-sectoral responses that effectively contain outbreaks. In contrast, many low-income and resource-limited countries in sub-Saharan Africa and parts of Asia face challenges such as insufficient diagnostic capacity, reliance on culling alone, fragmented implementation, and socio-economic barriers that reduce overall NPCS effectiveness. Community resistance, logistical constraints, and inconsistent international coordination further complicate control efforts. The findings highlight the necessity of tailoring NPCS to local conditions, strengthening cross-sectoral collaboration, investing in diagnostic capacity and targeted vaccination, and adopting a holistic One Health framework to enhance outbreak response. Ultimately, sustainable, context-adapted non-pharmaceutical interventions coupled with pharmaceutical advances and socio-economic equity are essential to prevent and mitigate future HPAI epidemics globally. © 2025 The Author(s)
