AB  - Poultry eggs are a critical source of protein, vitamins, and minerals for people worldwide; facing the current global egg shortage is a significant concern. The shortage results from multifactorial causes, including avian influenza outbreaks, shifting consumer demand, and supply chain disruptions caused by the COVID-19 pandemic. The economic crisis caused by the pandemic has also impacted the availability and affordability of eggs, particularly in low-income countries. Notably, egg prices in the United States surged from $1.93 per dozen in 2019 to $4.95 in 2024—a 156 % increase—while Nigeria saw prices rise from $13.30 to $27.50 per dozen within a year. Taiwan’s laying hen population fell from 45 million to 33 million, contributing to a domestic supply shortfall of 500,000–800,000 eggs daily. These disruptions also threaten public health infrastructure, as over 80 % of influenza vaccines rely on egg-based production. One Health (OH), an approach which emphasizes the interconnectedness of human, animal, and environmental health, provides a comprehensive framework for understanding and addressing the egg shortage. OH approach to the egg shortage involves collaboration between agriculture and environmental sectors to address the root causes of the lack and ensure the sustainable production and distribution of eggs. Applying OH principles enable coordinated action across sectors to stabilize production, enhance disease surveillance, and support global food security. This study integrates OH perspectives with economic and policy analyses to identify sustainable pathways toward resolving the egg shortage crisis. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
