AB  - Containing epidemics of Avian Influenza Viruses (AIVs) is a challenge because AIVs continuously mutate introducing genetic and antigenic changes. Efficacy of in vivo sampling and analysis is often degraded and delayed due to inherent complexities and costs. Consequently, such in vivo analysis is enhanced using computational approaches, such as phylodynamic modeling and simulation. Phylodynamics aims to simultaneously model, simulate, and analyze the interrelated phenotypic, genotypic, and epidemiological processes that occur in nature. This paper presents our extended approach for phylodynamic modeling and simulation of AIV Specifically, we focus on modeling and simulation of multiple species of high risk waterfowl and environmental factors. In addition, we present results from case studies using real world data sets from 3 high risk countries, namely: Turkey, Nigeria, and Vietnam. The paper also discusses results from generalized sensitivity analyses conducted to identify influential ecological and epidemiological parameters. The influential parameters inform containment and prophylaxis strategies to mitigate epidemics in livestock and reduce socioeconomic losses.
