AB  - The incursion of Highly Pathogenic Avian Influenza (HPAI) virus, subtype H5N1 into poultry farms in West Africa began in January 2006. The virus was introduced first to Nigeria as a consequence of the global spread of Gs/Gd/96 lineage of H5Nx from South East China most likely through migratory wild birds. HPAI was widely transmitted throughout West Africa mainly by cross-country activity of these birds and aggravated by trade in poultry and poultry products. Live bird market operations also played significant role in the dissemination of influenza virus along poultry value chain. Consequently the impact of HPAI on the poultry sub sector of the economy was severe with mortality in the range of 70 - 100% and subsequent depopulation of exposed birds as part of control measures. The virulence of HPAI manifests in per-acute disease of short incubation period and sometimes no clinical signs are noticeable before death. The clinical signs of influenza can be remarkable amongst the most susceptible species including chickens and turkeys. The highly pathogenic form of Avian influenza virus (AIV) affects all body systems, infected organs present clinical and pathological features of tissues damage ranging from haemorrhages to neurological signs. However some species like waterfowls are less susceptible and may be asymptomatic. Treatment of AIV is usually not recommended and vaccination against HPAI in West Africa is not yet officially approve. Hence biosecurity tops options for the control of AIV and to reduce the risk of zoonotic transmission. In this chapter, we described the clinical and pathological manifestations of HPAI as experienced firsthand in over a decade of infection in West Africa and emphasised effective control measures. © 2020 by Nova Science Publishers, Inc.
